Take back the echo filters of an output used before

When recording restarts for another output (a switch of speakers, the sound server moving
the stream), AEC3 starts its filters from nothing and the words that come in the first
second over the new echo lose more than over the old one. A call often goes back and forth
between two outputs, and the echo path of the one used before is the same as it was.

Now, at each restart, the filters in use (refined and coarse, of both the improved and the
reference echo removal), if they converged since the last flush, are kept with the delay
they were aligned to and their ERLE estimates, in one of two slots: the slot with the same
delay whose impulse response matches (normalized correlation of at least 0.9), or else the
one kept least recently. The filters just kept are not tested, since the old output's echo
still sounds for its delay. Those of the other slots are tested at every restart, also after
a headset, whose filters are not kept, once the delays of both outputs have passed (4 blocks
more), without changing the filters in use: the capture is correlated with the echo they
estimate at their delay, at lags of up to two blocks either way (an output can come back a
few milliseconds off), and at 4 and 6 blocks off as a control. They fit when the capture
holds that estimate at nearly its scale (0.8 to 1.25: a louder echo, the volume raised
since, would come through the suppression set for them) and not far below it (-10 dB), the
best lag is not within 16 samples of the edge of those tested, the correlation at the best
lag is at least twice that at the other delays, and no lag 16 samples or more away comes
within 0.7 of it (music and other tonal sound correlates at many lags, and then neither the
lag nor the delay can be told). The test counts blocks with capture and render and decides
after 6 if the correlation is 0.9 or more, otherwise after 12, before nearend speech is
likely; it follows the render it tests when the delay estimator moves the alignment, and
stops at a buffer flush or after 250 blocks.

Filters that do not fit at their delay are tested again, up to twice in the 2 s after the
restart, at a delay the delay estimator has found since the restart, when that is two blocks
or more from the one tested: an output's own buffering can move it further than the test
looks. At such a delay, to which music and other tonal sound can draw the delay estimator at
a wrong peak, no lag 16 samples or more away may come within 0.6 of the best.

On a fit, the render is aligned to their delay, the filters are shifted by the lag found
(whole blocks into the delay, the rest within the filters), they and their ERLE are taken
back, and the alignment is held for 250 blocks (ended by a flush or a render underrun).
After the hold, a delay found within a block of it is taken for the same echo (the delay
search rounds it either way), and the render is not aligned to it: that would set the
filters taken back aside, a second or so after taking them back, in 2 to 4 of 7 changes per
run with outputs up to 30 ms off. The filters, the ERLE and the suppressor then leave their
initial state for good, as they would once converged (the render counted until its end is
taken as seen): otherwise its end, 2.5 s of render later, sets the ERLE taken back to
nothing, and the words starting then are suppressed more than without this change. The delay
the render is aligned to is noted with every delay applied, also one where the buffer
already stood (a delay given from outside). Logs "kept filters tested, ... taken back" and
"filters of delay N taken back".

Measured in an AEC3-only simulation (16 kHz, 48 runs of 8 changes in double talk, echo +1.8
dB over the words; against the same runs without this change; dt0.5/dt3.5 are the mean loss
of a word starting 0.5 s and 3.5 s after the change, over the same stretch with no change):
- back and forth between two outputs (77 and 193 ms, other rooms): dt0.5 2.05 -> 0.46 dB,
  dt3.5 0.29 -> 0.01, words more than 6 dB down 0.9 % -> 0; another room at the same delay
  1.94 -> 1.26; another room only 1.85 -> 0.45. The device's delay off by 3 ms each time
  2.21 -> 1.04, by up to 30 ms 2.03 -> 1.23. Without the played sound being kept 2.05 ->
  0.68. From a headset back to speakers 0.72 -> 0.31. With the previous change that keeps
  the played sound at a short gap: 2.07 -> 0.46 (outside delay 40 ms: 2.09 -> 0.44), from a
  headset back to speakers 0.86 -> 0.34; the same output kept, the same to the sample.
- Of 384 words per case, none loses more than 1 dB at 0.5 s. At 3.5 s, by the error to word
  ratio, 0 or 1 lose more than 1 dB, fewer than a change of 0.01 dB in the far gain alone
  gives (4 to 11).
- music and unmuting: the same to the sample. Echo bursts at a restart the same or lower; in
  a silent room, after a change with no filters taken back, the output of the first half
  second can be 0.1 of the smallest 16-bit step instead of zero.
